<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lateran Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lateran Pacts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lateran Treaty was signed on 11 February 1929 by Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-gasparri">Pietro Gasparri</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. It settled the Roman Question, the dispute that had followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1870, by creating the independent state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a>. An accompanying concordat regulated the position of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty.jpg" alt="Lateran Treaty" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Group_of_Vatican_and_Italian_government_notables_posing_at_the_Lateran_Palace_before_the_signing_of_the_treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the mid-19th century the Papal States under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a> resisted incorporation into the new nation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> in 1860, leaving the pope only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>, and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in 1870. For the following sixty years relations between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the Italian government were hostile.</p><p>Under the Law of Guarantees of 1871 the Italian government offered Pius IX and his successors the use, but not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, of the Vatican and Lateran Palaces and a yearly income of 3,250,000 lire. The Holy See refused, on the grounds that the pope's spiritual jurisdiction required clear independence from any political power, and each pope thereafter considered himself a prisoner in the Vatican.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Negotiations began in 1926 between the Holy See and the fascist government led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>. The agreements take their name from the Lateran Palace, where they were signed on 11 February 1929, and the Italian Parliament ratified them on 7 June 1929. They are often presented as three instruments: a treaty of 27 articles, a financial convention of three articles and a concordat of 45 articles.</p><p>The political treaty recognised the full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the Holy See in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a>. The pope was pledged to perpetual neutrality in international relations and to abstention from mediation unless all parties requested it. In its first article <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> reaffirmed the principle of the 1848 constitution that the Catholic, Apostolic and Roman religion was the only religion of the state. The financial convention settled all claims of the Holy See arising from the loss of the Papal States, for a sum less than Italy had offered in 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Italian racial laws of 1938 prohibited marriages between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and non-Jews, including Catholics. The Vatican regarded this as a violation of the concordat, which specified that marriages performed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> would always be considered valid by civil authorities.</p><p>The constitution of the Italian Republic, adopted in 1948, states that relations between the state and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> are regulated by the Lateran Treaties. In 1984 the concordat was significantly revised, and both sides declared that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> as the sole religion of the Italian state was no longer in force. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-city">Vatican City</a> remains a distinct sovereign entity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lateran Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Italian troops entered Rome in 1870 through a breach in the walls at Porta Pia, and the popes then lived as self-declared prisoners in the Vatican until the <b>Lateran Treaty</b> of 1929.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Their loss in 1870 freed the papacy from the burdens of territorial government and, after the <b>Lateran Treaty</b> of 1929, left it with the small sovereign state of Vatican City, which allows the Holy See to act in international affairs as a sovereign entity today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Vatican City was created by the <b>Lateran Treaty</b> of 1929; Monaco has been ruled by the Grimaldi family since 1297, with brief interruptions; and Singapore, which left the Federation of Malaysia in 1965, has been called by The Economist the “world’s only fully functioning city-state”.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Papal States lost most of their lands to Italian unification in 1860 and Rome itself on 20 September 1870; the papacy refused to accept the loss until the <b>Lateran Treaty</b> of 1929 created Vatican City.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Question was settled by the <b>Lateran Treaty</b> of 11 February 1929, signed by Cardinal Pietro Gasparri for Pope Pius XI and Benito Mussolini for the Kingdom of Italy, creating Vatican City.</blockquote><a href="/state/vatican-city">Vatican City</a></li></ul></section>
